```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: super_spine_router_0
diagnosis_text:
The zebra daemon on super_spine_router_0 is dead. Its PID file (/run/frr/zebra.pid)
still exists holding PID 50, but that process is gone — confirmed by `kill -0 50`
returning "No such process." The frr.service unit is reported as inactive (dead),
meaning the service-level supervisor has also gone down.

The watchfrr watchdog, which is responsible for detecting and restarting crashed FRR
daemons, is likewise absent: its PID file is 0 bytes (empty, timestamped 06:50 —
two minutes after the lab started), and no watchfrr process appears in the process
table. Without watchfrr, there is no self-healing mechanism to bring zebra back up
automatically.

The symptom pattern — "daemon-connection errors" from the audit tool and inability
to read interface state via the FRR CLI — is the direct consequence of zebra being
absent. Zebra is the FRR core daemon: it owns the zserv API socket, supplies routing
table and interface data to vtysh, and serves as the bus between protocol daemons and
the kernel. Every vtysh query for routes or interfaces reports "zebra is not running."

Meanwhile, bgpd survived as an orphaned process and continues to maintain its BGP TCP
sessions to both spine routers purely through its own keepalive machinery, which is why
spine_router_0_0 and spine_router_0_1 still show the super-spine peer as Established.
Data-plane forwarding is also intact because kernel routes installed before zebra died
remain in the FIB until explicitly removed.

Root cause: the zebra daemon (and its watchdog watchfrr) were terminated on
super_spine_router_0, leaving frr.service dead with no self-recovery.

Fix: run `systemctl restart frr` on super_spine_router_0. This will relaunch
watchfrr, zebra, and all enabled daemons from /etc/frr/daemons, restore the vtysh
management plane, and bring the frr.service back to an active (running) state.
=== END ===
